# Code Review Rule

## Self-Review Before PR

The agent performs a structured self-review of all changes before creating a PR.
This is not optional. Run through every section below.

## Review Checklist

### 1. Correctness
- [ ] Implementation matches the acceptance criteria from the ticket
- [ ] All edge cases identified in requirements are handled
- [ ] No off-by-one errors in loops or index operations
- [ ] Async operations properly awaited; no unhandled promise rejections
- [ ] Null/undefined/None properly guarded before access
- [ ] Return types match declared signatures

### 2. Code Quality
- [ ] No function longer than 10 lines (project default; override in project.yaml)
- [ ] No class with more than one responsibility
- [ ] No duplicated logic (DRY — extract if 3+ repetitions)
- [ ] No magic numbers or strings — use named constants
- [ ] No dead code, commented-out code, or debug statements left in
- [ ] No `TODO` without a ticket reference (temporary TODOs must reference a Jira/GitHub issue)
- [ ] Imports are clean — no unused imports

### 3. Security
- [ ] User input is validated/sanitized at entry points
- [ ] No secrets, credentials, or API keys in code or config files
- [ ] SQL queries use parameterized statements (no string concatenation)
- [ ] Authentication/authorization checked on all protected endpoints
- [ ] Sensitive data not logged
- [ ] Dependencies do not have known CVEs (check `npm audit` / `pip-audit` / `gradle dependencyCheckAnalyze`)

### 4. Performance
- [ ] No N+1 query problems (batching/eager-loading used where needed)
- [ ] Expensive operations cached where appropriate
- [ ] No blocking synchronous operations in async contexts
- [ ] Large data sets paginated, not loaded entirely into memory
- [ ] New database queries have appropriate indexes

### 5. Maintainability
- [ ] Variable and function names are intention-revealing
- [ ] Complex logic has a single-line explanation comment (WHY, not WHAT)
- [ ] New abstractions follow the project's existing patterns
- [ ] No circular dependencies introduced
- [ ] Migrations are reversible (down migration provided)

### 6. Test Coverage and AC Traceability
- [ ] **Every AC in the requirements doc has at least one test** — verify by searching for each `[TICKET-ID]-AC-N` in the test suite
  - Run: `grep -r "TICKET-ID-AC-" tests/` (or equivalent path)
  - Any AC with 0 hits = **BLOCKER**
- [ ] AC IDs are embedded in test description strings (not just in comments)
- [ ] Traceability Matrix in `docs/designs/TICKET-ID-requirements.md` is filled in and up to date
- [ ] All new code has corresponding tests
- [ ] Tests cover happy path, error cases, and edge cases
- [ ] No test logic bypassed (`skip`, `only`, `xfail`) without ticket reference

### 7. Documentation
- [ ] Public API changes reflected in docs/API.md or OpenAPI spec
- [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated if this is a user-facing change
- [ ] README updated if setup/usage steps changed

## Automated Review Commands

Run these before creating the PR (commands from `config/project.yaml`):
```
1. Linter:    [project linter command]
2. Formatter: [project formatter command] --check
3. Tests:     [project test command]
4. Type check: [project typecheck command]
5. Security:  [project audit command]
```

## Review Output Format

After self-review, produce:
```markdown
## Code Review Summary

### Issues Found
- [BLOCKER] Description of issue and file:line
- [WARNING] Description of issue and file:line

### Approved
- Correctness: ✅
- Security: ✅
- Tests: ✅ (87% coverage)
- Quality: ⚠️ (1 warning - see above)

### Ready for PR: YES / NO
```

If any BLOCKER issues exist, fix them before creating the PR.
